Agent credential seeding
========================

scripts/agent-credentials.conf is the list of portable CLI credential files that scripts/deploy-agent-credentials.sh can seed from Bitwarden Secrets Manager.
One tool per line.
Fields are separated by tabs: tool, secret key, target path, mode.
Blank lines are ignored.
A line whose first non-whitespace character is # is a comment.
The target path is relative to $HOME, never absolute.
The mode is a four-digit octal permission such as 0600.

commandcode uses AGENT_COMMANDCODE_AUTH_JSON at .commandcode/auth.json, mode 0600.
That secret already lives in the phillias project (fe9eea09-9eaf-45e6-a72f-b45e0150b29d), the same project id scripts/setup.sh uses.
Its secret id is 3a49387f-5c35-477f-80ad-b4fd0002d116.
codex uses AGENT_CODEX_AUTH_JSON at .codex/auth.json, mode 0600.
claude uses AGENT_CLAUDE_CREDENTIALS_JSON at .claude/.credentials.json, mode 0600.
Claude's target is a dotfile inside .claude/.
opencode uses AGENT_OPENCODE_AUTH_JSON at .local/share/opencode/auth.json, mode 0600.
OpenCode's target is two directories under .local/share/.
The deployer creates those parents with mkdir -p and does not strip a leading dot.
That path used to be chezmoi-managed from an age-encrypted source.
That source is gone, and .chezmoiignore lists the target so chezmoi does not manage it.
Apply does not delete an existing copy.
A host that still has the old encrypted snapshot must not treat that file as the opencode seed.
Move the old file aside and re-create the credential on that host from BWS, because the encrypted copy is a stale snapshot.
Deploy-once skips a file that is already present, so the old copy blocks the new seed until it is moved.
Do not commit the credential value.
The value of each secret is the base64 encoding of the credential file and lives only in BWS.
Do not commit the value, a decoded copy, or a backup of the file.
The codex, claude, and opencode secrets are created by the captain.
The deployer tolerates a missing secret: that row prints `tool error missing-secret`, later rows still run, and the process exits nonzero if any row failed.

grok uses AGENT_GROK_AUTH_JSON at .grok/auth.json, mode 0600.
Its credential-file portability is verified (dotfiles#589).
BWS secret id f199afaf-6eb1-4c2c-9410-b4ff001c2cf6.

Adding a tool: create the BWS secret in that phillias project first, with the file's base64 as the value and a key named AGENT_<TOOL>_AUTH_JSON, then append one manifest line.
This change does not wire the deployer into setup.sh.
Leave a tool out of the manifest until its credential file is verified portable.

Refresh staleness: the vault snapshot is a first-login seed, not a live source.
The deployer writes a target only when that file is absent.
After seeding, that host's own CLI refreshes the credential through its normal token refresh.
Running the deployer again prints "deploy-once: exists, skipping (use --force)" and leaves the file alone.
Overwriting a live credential requires explicit --force, because the local CLI holds newer refresh tokens than any vault snapshot.
--force overwrites only after writing a timestamped backup beside the target.
A stale snapshot costs one extra login on a brand-new host.
It is not a continuous sync, and there is no periodic re-upload here.

Fallback when BWS is unreachable or the snapshot is too old to refresh: log in with the tool on a laptop and scp the credential file to the host path, mode 0600.
That laptop-login plus scp path is the manual procedure this seed replaces for a fresh host.

The deployer prints one stdout line per entry: tool, status, path, and a 12-hex sha256 prefix.
A failed row prints `tool error <code>` and does not stop the remaining rows.
It never prints file contents.

Chezmoi applies scripts/* into ~/scripts/ with the executable bit set when the source filename has the executable_ prefix (PR #610).
The deploy script source is scripts/executable_deploy-agent-credentials.sh and renders at mode 0775.

commandcode whoami hangs on kali (CabinInspiron22Kali): it times out around 30-45 seconds, and it returns quickly on kalione.
That is a known variance of the CLI, not of this seed.
Do not treat a hang there as a failed deploy.
Auth proof for commandcode should use quota-axi, not commandcode whoami.
bws 2.1.0 `secret get` accepts a secret id, not a key name, so the script resolves the manifest key inside the phillias project and then fetches the bytes with `bws secret get`.
